WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.779
[Narrator] Why would a refrigerator
company order helium from the Moon?

2
00:00:04.043 --> 00:00:07.757
[Thad] I’m Thad Connelly.
This is The Quantum Distillery.

3
00:00:08.103 --> 00:00:13.119
[Narrator] In September 2025,
Bluefors agreed to buy up to 10,000 liters

4
00:00:13.119 --> 00:00:18.633
of helium-3 a year from Interlune,
which plans to recover it from lunar soil.

5
00:00:19.156 --> 00:00:22.127
Deliveries are supposed to start in 2028.

6
00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.356
Bringing it back is still ahead of them.

7
00:00:24.507 --> 00:00:26.876
The refrigerators already exist.

8
00:00:26.969 --> 00:00:30.440
And if you want to understand that order,
follow the helium.

9
00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:35.525
There are two pieces of hardware
to keep in mind while we follow this.

10
00:00:35.676 --> 00:00:38.231
One fits in the palm of your hand.

11
00:00:38.382 --> 00:00:41.098
It's the package holding
a quantum processor.

12
00:00:41.249 --> 00:00:43.374
The other can occupy a lab.

13
00:00:43.595 --> 00:00:47.565
It creates the conditions in
which that first piece can do any work.

14
00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:51.791
You can learn a surprising amount
about the future of quantum computing

15
00:00:51.826 --> 00:00:54.554
from the person who has
to make room for both.

16
00:00:54.647 --> 00:00:57.457
That person has to figure out
where to put the pumps,

17
00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:02.170
how to get signals in, and how to tell
whether the machine did what you asked.

18
00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:06.013
Last time we counted the qubits
it might take to break encryption.

19
00:01:06.106 --> 00:01:10.564
Today, look at the famous photograph
of a superconducting quantum computer.

20
00:01:10.657 --> 00:01:13.072
Gold plates, loops of cable,

21
00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:16.322
something that looks like
a chandelier hung upside down.

22
00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:18.133
Where's the processor?

23
00:01:18.168 --> 00:01:22.591
Follow the cables to the bottom. The chip
occupies a small package down there.

24
00:01:22.684 --> 00:01:25.413
Much of what you're looking
at is the refrigerator.

25
00:01:25.506 --> 00:01:30.207
The photograph usually shows it open,
so we can see inside. To run the machine,

26
00:01:30.207 --> 00:01:34.572
the team puts shields and a vacuum
enclosure around those layers.

27
00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:38.636
Picture a cross section through
a series of nested metal cans.

28
00:01:38.729 --> 00:01:42.374
The outermost one faces
a room full of warm surfaces.

29
00:01:42.467 --> 00:01:45.857
Shields farther in see
colder surroundings.

30
00:01:45.892 --> 00:01:49.387
Cables have to get through all of it.
And the chip at the bottom needs

31
00:01:49.387 --> 00:01:53.009
a good thermal connection
to that last cold stage.

32
00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.979
A thermometer on a metal plate can
tell you the temperature of the plate.

33
00:01:57.386 --> 00:02:00.173
Finding out what the qubit
is actually experiencing

34
00:02:00.173 --> 00:02:02.970
takes a measurement of the qubit itself.

35
00:02:02.971 --> 00:02:05.990
Inside that chip,
two energy levels of a tiny

36
00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:09.391
electrical circuit stand for zero and one.

37
00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:14.081
A pulse of microwave energy lets
you change the state deliberately.

38
00:02:14.175 --> 00:02:18.691
Heat can give the circuit enough
energy to change state all by itself.

39
00:02:19.098 --> 00:02:22.383
Suppose you tell it to start
a calculation at zero.

40
00:02:22.534 --> 00:02:24.577
If it's already bounced up to one,

41
00:02:24.577 --> 00:02:28.211
you've got the wrong answer
to a question you haven't even asked yet.

42
00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:33.563
Cooling makes those accidental
excitations much less likely.

43
00:02:34.225 --> 00:02:39.670
Take a representative superconducting
qubit that responds at 5 GHz.

44
00:02:39.891 --> 00:02:43.176
5 billion cycles per second sounds fast,

45
00:02:43.176 --> 00:02:46.787
but a photon at that frequency
carries very little energy.

46
00:02:47.007 --> 00:02:50.664
Express the gap between zero
and one on a temperature scale

47
00:02:50.664 --> 00:02:52.800
and it's about a quarter of a kelvin.

48
00:02:52.951 --> 00:02:55.807
A room is roughly 300 kelvin.

49
00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:59.836
So the chip has to sit far
below that quarter-kelvin scale,

50
00:02:59.836 --> 00:03:03.876
and its electrical surroundings
have to be quiet at that frequency.

51
00:03:04.027 --> 00:03:07.371
You could have an excellent refrigerator
and still spoil an experiment

52
00:03:07.371 --> 00:03:10.773
by giving the warm room a direct
microwave path to the chip.

53
00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:15.614
That explains some of those little
boxes mounted along the cables.

54
00:03:15.615 --> 00:03:18.355
Now count the plates from top to bottom.

55
00:03:18.506 --> 00:03:22.290
Each stage intercepts heat
before it reaches the next.

56
00:03:22.813 --> 00:03:26.064
At the cold end,
a dilution refrigerator can get within

57
00:03:26.064 --> 00:03:29.094
a hundredth of a degree of absolute zero.

58
00:03:29.501 --> 00:03:32.786
The last step uses two forms of helium.

59
00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:38.695
Where the liquids meet, helium-3 can
pass into a mixture rich in helium-4.

60
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:41.285
That takes energy from the surroundings.

61
00:03:41.285 --> 00:03:45.476
Pumps keep the helium circulating
so the cooling continues.

62
00:03:45.696 --> 00:03:48.134
The same helium goes around again.

63
00:03:48.227 --> 00:03:51.373
Each additional fridge
needs its own supply.

64
00:03:51.466 --> 00:03:53.742
Helium-3 is an isotope.

65
00:03:53.835 --> 00:03:59.117
Compared with the common helium-4 atom,
its nucleus has one fewer neutron.

66
00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:03.552
That small difference changes how the two
liquids behave near absolute zero

67
00:04:03.552 --> 00:04:06.129
and makes this cooling cycle possible.

68
00:04:06.652 --> 00:04:11.516
Bluefors actually lists the gas
quantities for different refrigerators.

69
00:04:11.667 --> 00:04:15.626
For one model,
the listed charge is 40 liters of helium-3

70
00:04:15.626 --> 00:04:18.459
and 180 liters of helium-4.

71
00:04:18.773 --> 00:04:22.488
Those are gas quantities,
measured as gas volumes.

72
00:04:22.709 --> 00:04:26.563
Once the system is running,
it circulates a fixed inventory.

73
00:04:26.656 --> 00:04:29.930
So when a manufacturer
lines up a future source,

74
00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.495
think about outfitting
a fleet of machines.

75
00:04:32.809 --> 00:04:37.081
A year's worth of computing on one
machine is a different question.

76
00:04:37.082 --> 00:04:40.216
So we've put the chip in a quiet,
cold place.

77
00:04:40.368 --> 00:04:42.108
Now we have to talk to it.

78
00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:45.278
Follow one control cable
from the electronics rack,

79
00:04:45.278 --> 00:04:49.202
which is warm enough to rest your hand on,
all the way down to the chip.

80
00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:53.532
It carries the microwave instruction.
It also conducts heat.

81
00:04:53.754 --> 00:04:57.260
Engineers fasten the cable
to successive cold stages

82
00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.918
and reduce the noise coming down the line.

83
00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:04.666
Even when the computer is sitting idle,
that cable is still bringing heat in.

84
00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.058
A team in Zurich measured what was

85
00:05:07.058 --> 00:05:09.950
available at the bottom
of a particular fridge.

86
00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:16.974
With one circulation setting they could
remove about 5 microwatts at 13 mK.

87
00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:20.468
Let that stage warm to 20 mK

88
00:05:20.562 --> 00:05:24.090
and it could remove about 13 microwatts.

89
00:05:24.242 --> 00:05:27.260
Same fridge. Same circulation setting.

90
00:05:27.574 --> 00:05:31.938
Two different answers to the question,
how much cooling do I get?

91
00:05:32.948 --> 00:05:36.618
The engineers also measured
the heat conducted by cables

92
00:05:36.618 --> 00:05:40.332
and the heat released while electrical
signals were running through them.

93
00:05:40.484 --> 00:05:43.176
If you're adding a bundle
of lines to that machine,

94
00:05:43.212 --> 00:05:47.078
you need both sets of numbers
before you drill another hole.

95
00:05:47.078 --> 00:05:50.201
Imagine adding a hundred
more control connections.

96
00:05:50.352 --> 00:05:53.196
Where do you put them,
and how do you cool them?

97
00:05:53.858 --> 00:05:56.308
A Delft company called FrostByte is

98
00:05:56.308 --> 00:05:59.617
developing switches that
work inside the refrigerator.

99
00:05:59.837 --> 00:06:03.007
The idea is to send
signals down fewer lines

100
00:06:03.007 --> 00:06:05.363
and route them closer to the processor.

101
00:06:05.677 --> 00:06:09.566
Put electronics in the cold
and you face the next question.

102
00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:13.026
Those electronics generate
heat of their own.

103
00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:17.438
How much can you dissipate on the stage
where the switch sits?

104
00:06:17.438 --> 00:06:20.154
That's a design decision
you can measure in the lab.

105
00:06:20.305 --> 00:06:24.415
Another team actually sent a control
signal down an optical fiber.

106
00:06:24.636 --> 00:06:27.353
At the cold end a photodetector converted

107
00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:31.532
modulated laser light into a microwave
signal the circuit could use.

108
00:06:32.055 --> 00:06:34.679
The researchers demonstrated
that they could control

109
00:06:34.679 --> 00:06:37.906
and read a superconducting
qubit over that link.

110
00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.608
One qubit. That experiment
answered a specific question.

111
00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:46.788
Could the conversion preserve a signal
clean enough to operate the device?

112
00:06:47.195 --> 00:06:51.839
A machine with many such links raises
additional questions about total optical

113
00:06:51.839 --> 00:06:57.109
power, heat from the conversion,
and how many signals can share each fiber.

114
00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:01.440
A working experiment gives you
a place to start measuring all three.

115
00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:06.118
Walk into a neutral atom lab and you
can build a very different picture.

116
00:07:06.338 --> 00:07:09.450
Here an individual atom
carries the information.

117
00:07:09.544 --> 00:07:13.594
It's moving through a vacuum chamber,
and you'd like it to stay put.

118
00:07:13.908 --> 00:07:16.486
Laser light slows the atoms down.

119
00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:21.896
A tightly focused beam can hold one
in a tiny spot, an optical tweezer.

120
00:07:22.118 --> 00:07:26.354
Make an array of those spots,
and you can arrange the atoms in rows.

121
00:07:26.506 --> 00:07:29.780
The atoms themselves get
extraordinarily cold.

122
00:07:29.872 --> 00:07:32.972
The chamber around them can
stay at room temperature.

123
00:07:33.634 --> 00:07:37.268
Look at what the Caltech team did in 2025.

124
00:07:37.302 --> 00:07:43.572
They made about 12,000 trap sites
and loaded more than 6,100 neutral atoms.

125
00:07:43.724 --> 00:07:46.928
A trap site is a place
the optics can hold an atom.

126
00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.934
A loaded site actually contains one.

127
00:07:50.086 --> 00:07:53.382
Take a picture and you can see
which seats are occupied.

128
00:07:53.696 --> 00:07:57.284
In the same work the researchers
measured how well the atomic qubits

129
00:07:57.284 --> 00:08:00.964
preserved phase information,
and they could pick up atoms,

130
00:08:00.964 --> 00:08:04.736
move them, and put them down
while preserving coherence.

131
00:08:05.144 --> 00:08:08.556
Getting that combination right
is what makes moving atoms around

132
00:08:08.556 --> 00:08:10.948
interesting for a future computation.

133
00:08:11.262 --> 00:08:16.312
The two counts tell you how many places
they made and how many atoms they caught.

134
00:08:16.312 --> 00:08:19.458
Light gives you a way
to read the answer, too.

135
00:08:19.609 --> 00:08:23.672
Shine light tuned to a particular
atomic transition.

136
00:08:23.893 --> 00:08:27.330
An atom in the state you're
looking for scatters photons,

137
00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:30.197
and a lens collects some
of them for a camera.

138
00:08:30.418 --> 00:08:34.238
On the image you can pick
out individual bright sites.

139
00:08:34.389 --> 00:08:39.079
Now the shopping list looks different.
A laser that holds its frequency.

140
00:08:39.079 --> 00:08:41.796
Optics that focus it on the right site.

141
00:08:41.889 --> 00:08:45.151
A camera sensitive enough
to collect that faint signal.

142
00:08:45.674 --> 00:08:48.077
There's a lovely practical catch.

143
00:08:48.112 --> 00:08:51.409
To see an atom clearly,
you'd like to collect plenty of light.

144
00:08:51.444 --> 00:08:55.821
Scattering light kicks the atom, and
enough kicks can knock it out of its trap.

145
00:08:55.856 --> 00:09:00.244
You want an image and you'd like
your atom back when the exposure ends.

146
00:09:00.767 --> 00:09:05.759
The Caltech group reported better
than 99.99% imaging fidelity

147
00:09:05.794 --> 00:09:08.116
and measured survival separately.

148
00:09:08.638 --> 00:09:11.680
Try measuring both
on an array of thousands.

149
00:09:11.831 --> 00:09:14.734
A camera that confidently
reports an empty site

150
00:09:14.734 --> 00:09:19.598
and an atom that actually left the site
can give you the same dark pixel.

151
00:09:19.912 --> 00:09:23.661
Figuring out which event happened
is part of building the instrument.

152
00:09:23.661 --> 00:09:25.588
Here's a question for a supplier.

153
00:09:25.739 --> 00:09:28.944
If the big quantum computer
comes five years late,

154
00:09:28.944 --> 00:09:31.277
who still needs your equipment?

155
00:09:32.287 --> 00:09:37.245
At the US national standards lab,
researchers use a dilution refrigerator

156
00:09:37.245 --> 00:09:43.282
to cool a radiation detector below
0.05 degrees above absolute zero.

157
00:09:43.502 --> 00:09:47.960
A single radioactive decay
deposits a tiny pulse of heat.

158
00:09:48.181 --> 00:09:52.210
From that pulse they can work out
how much energy the decay released.

159
00:09:52.430 --> 00:09:55.217
That's an instrument doing a job today.

160
00:09:55.623 --> 00:09:58.630
There's a second customer
for precision optics, too.

161
00:09:59.036 --> 00:10:00.941
In an optical atomic clock,

162
00:10:00.941 --> 00:10:04.946
scientists tune a laser
to a transition inside an atom.

163
00:10:05.039 --> 00:10:09.195
The transition gives them a reference
for how fast the light is oscillating.

164
00:10:09.509 --> 00:10:13.259
The challenge is holding that laser
steady enough to compare its frequency

165
00:10:13.259 --> 00:10:16.068
with the atoms over and over again.

166
00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:21.653
The national standards lab runs
clocks using laser-cooled atoms

167
00:10:21.688 --> 00:10:23.789
held in a lattice of light.

168
00:10:24.614 --> 00:10:26.842
Think back to the atom computer.

169
00:10:26.994 --> 00:10:31.069
People in those two labs can have
a very specific conversation about laser

170
00:10:31.069 --> 00:10:34.342
stability, optics and control electronics.

171
00:10:34.435 --> 00:10:37.489
They can need some of the same
engineering even when only one

172
00:10:37.489 --> 00:10:39.741
of them wants to run an algorithm.

173
00:10:39.741 --> 00:10:43.967
So go back to the Moon deal
and read the words 'up to.'

174
00:10:44.373 --> 00:10:47.160
They matter as much
as the number beside them.

175
00:10:47.311 --> 00:10:51.188
A customer has put a figure and a delivery
window on what it might buy.

176
00:10:51.281 --> 00:10:54.752
A space company still has to collect
the gas and bring it home.

177
00:10:54.787 --> 00:10:57.666
And the atom lab we just visited can order

178
00:10:57.666 --> 00:11:02.717
another set of lasers without moving
that helium order by a single liter.

179
00:11:03.239 --> 00:11:08.313
To evaluate one of these suppliers,
ask to see a customer acceptance test.

180
00:11:08.626 --> 00:11:11.505
For a refrigerator,
what temperature did it hold

181
00:11:11.505 --> 00:11:15.452
with the customer's wiring installed
and the signals turned on?

182
00:11:15.859 --> 00:11:19.203
For a laser,
how long did it stay within the required

183
00:11:19.203 --> 00:11:21.757
frequency range during a real run?

184
00:11:22.279 --> 00:11:25.471
Then look at the second
customer's requirements.

185
00:11:25.785 --> 00:11:29.453
Sometimes a second lab needs
nearly the same piece of equipment.

186
00:11:29.547 --> 00:11:33.796
Sometimes it needs so much redesign
that it's effectively a second product.

187
00:11:34.109 --> 00:11:37.232
That difference tells you
much more about the business

188
00:11:37.232 --> 00:11:40.216
than a long list of industries on a slide.

189
00:11:40.217 --> 00:11:44.199
One last look at those two photographs.
On one side,

190
00:11:44.199 --> 00:11:48.622
a cable carries a microwave pulse
down through a stack of metal plates.

191
00:11:48.774 --> 00:11:53.917
On the other, a lens brings light to a
spot small enough to hold a single atom.

192
00:11:54.439 --> 00:11:58.851
In each photograph it's possible
to point to where the information sits.

193
00:11:59.002 --> 00:12:01.022
It's also possible to follow a signal all

194
00:12:01.022 --> 00:12:05.121
the way from an ordinary computer
to that spot and back again.

195
00:12:05.272 --> 00:12:09.323
That whole trip has to work
before anybody can run the algorithm.

196
00:12:10.148 --> 00:12:14.118
It brings the qubit count
from the last episode into perspective.

197
00:12:14.525 --> 00:12:19.412
The next time somebody shows you a quantum
chip, ask them to widen the shot.

198
00:12:19.819 --> 00:12:23.127
Where does the heat go?
What carries the instruction in?

199
00:12:23.163 --> 00:12:26.982
What carries the answer out?
Those questions will tell you

200
00:12:26.982 --> 00:12:30.372
a surprising amount about what
it takes to build the next one.

201
00:12:30.686 --> 00:12:33.519
This has been
The Machine Around the Machine.

202
00:12:33.612 --> 00:12:36.356
Thanks for joining us
at The Quantum Distillery.

203
00:12:46.725 --> 00:12:48.501
[Narrator] Sponsored by Gengyve.

204
00:12:48.826 --> 00:12:50.045
In the company’s words,

205
00:12:50.196 --> 00:12:52.785
“the world’s first quantum-inspired
frontier

206
00:12:52.902 --> 00:12:55.223
oral and systemic health company.”
